    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite is green \u2014 all 22 tests pass, including the new `Cp13Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on `POST /api/owners`, returned via `GET /api/owners/{id}`, defined as the number of existing owners sharing the same firstName and lastName (case-insensitively) *before* this create.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, the changes were:\n\n1. **New pipeline step** \u2014 `AssignNamesakeCount.java` (`function/owner/`): reads the built `@Val Owner`, counts existing owners from `ownerRepository.findAll()` whose firstName and lastName match case-insensitively, and stores the total via `owner.setNamesakeCount(...)`. It runs after `BuildOwner` (names present) and before `SaveOwner`, so the not-yet-persisted owner is never counted among its own namesakes.\n\n2. **Wiring** \u2014 `owners.POST.yml`: inserted a `countNamesakes` step (under `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `assignHousehold` and `save`.\n\n3. **Persistence** \u2014 added the `namesakeCount` field (`Integer`, `namesake_count` column) with getter/setter to the `Owner` entity, and the `namesake_count` column to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by the tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres for consistency). The value is stored so the later GET returns the count computed at create time.\n\n4. **API contract** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`; MapStruct auto-maps it by name onto the generated `OwnerDto`.",
